# Testing Rules

## Test Structure
- Use Jest and React Testing Library
- Write tests for user interactions, not implementation details
- Use descriptive test names
- Group related tests with describe blocks

## Component Testing
- Test component rendering
- Test user interactions (clicks, form submissions)
- Test error states and loading states
- Test accessibility features

```typescript
// ✅ Good test structure
describe('BlogPostForm', () => {
  it('should render form fields correctly', () => {
    render(<BlogPostForm title="Test" onSave={jest.fn()} />)
    
    expect(screen.getByLabelText(/title/i)).toBeInTheDocument()
    expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /save/i })).toBeInTheDocument()
  })
  
  it('should call onSave when form is submitted', async () => {
    const mockSave = jest.fn()
    render(<BlogPostForm title="Test" onSave={mockSave} />)
    
    await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/title/i), 'New Title')
    await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /save/i }))
    
    expect(mockSave).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({
      title: 'New Title'
    }))
  })
})
```

## API Testing
- Test all API endpoints
- Test error handling
- Test authentication requirements
- Use proper mocking for external services

## Coverage
- Aim for 80%+ code coverage
- Focus on critical business logic
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Use coverage reports to identify gaps